Secured and Monitored Web Infrastructure
==========================================

Overview
--------
This builds on the three-server distributed infrastructure
(load balancer + 2 servers with web server, app server, codebase, and
database) by adding security and monitoring: 3 firewalls, an SSL
certificate for HTTPS, and 3 monitoring clients.

Components added and why
-------------------------
- 3 firewalls (one in front of the load balancer, one on each of the
  2 servers): each component is protected individually, so that even
  if traffic reaches the network, unauthorized ports/protocols are
  blocked before reaching the actual services.
- 1 SSL certificate (installed on the load balancer, terminating
  HTTPS there): allows the website to be served securely over HTTPS,
  encrypting traffic between the user and the infrastructure.
- 3 monitoring clients (one on the load balancer, one on each server):
  each component reports its own metrics (CPU, memory, disk, traffic,
  etc.) to the monitoring service, giving visibility into the health
  of every part of the infrastructure.

What are firewalls for?
A firewall controls incoming and outgoing network traffic based on
defined security rules, blocking unauthorized access while allowing
legitimate traffic through - protecting the servers from malicious or
unwanted connections.

Why is the traffic served over HTTPS?
HTTPS encrypts the data exchanged between the user's browser and the
server, protecting it from eavesdropping, interception, or tampering
by third parties (e.g. on public Wi-Fi), and verifies the identity of
the server via the SSL certificate.

What is monitoring used for?
Monitoring is used to track the health, performance, and availability
of the infrastructure in real time - detecting issues (high CPU,
downtime, slow response times, errors) early so they can be addressed
before they significantly impact users.

How does the monitoring tool collect data?
A monitoring client (agent) is installed on each server/component. It
collects local metrics (CPU, memory, disk, network, custom app
metrics) and periodically sends (pushes) that data to the monitoring
service, where it can be visualized and alerted on.

Monitoring web server QPS (Queries Per Second)
To monitor QPS, you would set up a custom metric/data collector that
specifically tracks the number of requests the web server (Nginx)
handles per second, and send that custom metric to the monitoring
service (e.g. by parsing the Nginx logs or using a plugin that exposes
request-rate metrics) so it can be graphed and alerted on.

Issues with this infrastructure
--------------------------------
Why is terminating SSL at the load balancer level an issue?
Traffic between the load balancer and the backend servers is no
longer encrypted (it is decrypted at the load balancer), so if an
attacker gains access to the internal network, they can see that
internal traffic in plain text.

Why is having only one MySQL server capable of accepting writes an issue?
That single write-capable (Primary) database server is a SPOF: if it
fails, no new data can be written anywhere in the system until it is
restored or a Replica is manually/automatically promoted, causing a
service disruption for any write operation.

Why might having servers with all the same components be a problem?
When every server runs the web server, application server, and
database together, a spike in load or resource usage caused by one
component (e.g. the database) can degrade or crash the other
components on the same server (e.g. the web/app server), since they
are all competing for the same CPU, memory, and disk resources. It
also makes it harder to scale or secure each component independently.
